+++
path = "blog/link-share-load"
title = "What a shared link costs a two-core box"
date = 2026-07-29
description = "Both my sites went slow at once, then recovered on their own. It was a link shared in Messenger. Same server, same files: 111 requests a second on fresh connections, 199 on reused ones."
+++

Both sasin91.xyz and athletos.app felt slow at the same time, and then they were
fine again without me touching anything.

One of them is a static site generated by a small Rust binary. The other is a
SvelteKit app running in [FreeBSD jails](/blog/athletos-freebsd/). Different
stacks, different code, nothing shared between them at the application level.

## They resolve to the same address

Both point at `37.27.218.191`. One Hetzner CX23, about €7 a month: FreeBSD 15.1,
2 vCPU on an AMD EPYC-Rome, 3.9 GB of RAM.

That rules out a site-level cause before I open a log. Two unrelated
applications do not get slow together by coincidence.

## A theory that fit the shape but not the scope

My first idea was cache headers. The site sent no `Cache-Control` at all, so
browsers fall back to heuristic freshness, roughly 10% of the age since
`Last-Modified`. Right after a deploy that age is near zero, which makes the
heuristic lifetime near zero, so every navigation revalidates.

That explains the shape exactly: slow right after a deploy, fine an hour later.
It is also wrong, because it cannot explain why a _different site_ on a
different stack was slow at the same time.

A theory that explains the shape but not the scope is not the answer. The cache
issue was real and got fixed, but it was a separate problem that happened to
look like this one.

## It was a link share

The spike lined up with sharing a link to the site in Meta Messenger. That
triggers `facebookexternalhit`, which fetches the URL in a burst of parallel
connections.

Not a lot of requests. A lot of connections, all at once.

## The load test

[Vegeta](https://github.com/tsenart/vegeta), ramped, against a realistic click
path of six URLs, with a second attack probing athletos.app at 2 requests a
second so I could measure the collateral damage on the neighbour.

Two profiles: connections reused, which is what a browser does, and a fresh
connection per request, which is what a crawler does.

{title="fresh connection per request"}
```bash
rate   delivered   mean     p99     success   athletos.app p90
  25        24.7   146ms   234ms     100%     0.175s
  50        49.4   146ms   239ms     100%     0.173s
 100        41.1   248ms   1.16s    99.5%     1.16s
 200         111   663ms   8.34s    99.2%     1.16s
 400         216   1.37s     15s    94.0%     one 10s timeout
```

{title="connection reused"}
```bash
rate   delivered   mean     p90     success
  50        49.9    49ms    54ms    100%
 200         199    54ms    54ms    99.95%
 800         796   251ms   705ms    99.99%
```

## The connection is the bottleneck, not the file

At 200 requests a second, fresh connections delivered 111 a second at a 663 ms
mean. Reused connections delivered 199 a second at a 54 ms mean. Same server,
same files.

Serving the files was never the constraint. Setting up the connection was. The
ceiling sits between 50 and 100 new TLS handshakes a second.

## Queuing, not failing

At 100 rps the attack asked for 1000 requests and only 5 of them errored.
Throughput still fell to 41 a second, and the run took 14 extra seconds to drain
after the last request went out.

Nothing failed. Everything waited, and it waited on the server side.

::: warning
## The 400 rps row is muddied

242 of the failures in that run were my test client's own IPv6 dial errors, not
the server refusing anything. The row stays in the table, but 94% is not the
box's success rate.
:::

## There was nothing to tune

I went looking for a configuration win and did not find one.

The certificate is already ECDSA P-256. An RSA certificate would have been the
obvious culprit, since an RSA handshake costs the server far more. TLS 1.3, so a
handshake is one round trip instead of two. Session tickets with a 7-day
lifetime, so a client that comes back skips the expensive part.

It was already configured correctly. The ceiling is just two shared cores doing
asymmetric crypto.

## The fix is to do nothing

Across 4000 requests at 400 rps the box never returned a 5xx, never dropped a
connection, and recovered immediately. It got slower and then it stopped being
slower. It degraded gracefully, which is the behaviour I actually want from it.

A link share costs about a minute of slowness, a few times a year. Putting a CDN
in front to absorb that would mean terminating TLS somewhere else to solve a
problem that resolves itself. For a personal site and a small app, that is the
wrong trade.

## The number worth knowing

For a small box, requests per second is not the interesting capacity number.
New connections per second is, and on the same hardware the two differ by more
than an order of magnitude.

Browsers reuse connections. Crawlers do not. That is the whole gap.

Every number above came out of a run against the live box.
